export default [["q-fcps-phy4-01","A dilated ventricle needs more wall tension to generate the same intraventricular pressure. This follows from:",["The law of Laplace","Poiseuille's law","Starling's law of the capillary","Boyle's law"],"Wall tension equals pressure times radius divided by wall thickness (Laplace). A larger radius means more tension for the same pressure."],["q-fcps-phy4-02","A patient has a left ventricular end-diastolic volume of 140 mL and an end-systolic volume of 60 mL. What is the ejection fraction?",["57%","43%","67%","80%"],"Stroke volume is 80 mL, and 80 divided by 140 is about 57 percent."],["q-fcps-phy4-03","In a pressure-volume loop, an increase in afterload with unchanged preload and contractility causes:",["Decreased stroke volume with increased end-systolic volume","Increased stroke volume","Decreased end-diastolic volume","No change in the loop"],"A higher afterload makes the ventricle eject less, so stroke volume falls and end-systolic volume rises."],["q-fcps-phy4-04","An increase in contractility shifts the end-systolic pressure-volume relationship:",["Upwards and to the left","Downwards and to the right","To the right only","It does not change"],"Greater contractility gives higher developed pressure at any volume, so the end-systolic line becomes steeper and moves up and left."],["q-fcps-phy4-05","The 'v' wave of the jugular venous pulse represents:",["Venous filling of the right atrium against a closed tricuspid valve","Atrial contraction","Tricuspid valve bulging during isovolumetric contraction","Rapid ventricular filling"],"The v wave is passive filling of the right atrium during ventricular systole while the tricuspid valve is closed. Giant v waves occur in tricuspid regurgitation."],["q-fcps-phy4-06","Cannon 'a' waves in the neck are seen when:",["The atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve","There is atrial fibrillation","There is tricuspid stenosis","There is constrictive pericarditis"],"In complete heart block or ventricular tachycardia with AV dissociation the atria occasionally contract against a closed tricuspid valve, producing cannon a waves."],["q-fcps-phy4-07","A third heart sound (S3) is caused by:",["Rapid ventricular filling in early diastole","Atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle","Closure of the mitral valve","Opening of the aortic valve"],"S3 occurs in early diastole during rapid filling. It is normal in young people and in pregnancy, and it is abnormal in older adults with heart failure."],["q-fcps-phy4-08","A fourth heart sound (S4) is typically heard in:",["Left ventricular hypertrophy with a stiff ventricle","Healthy children","Dilated cardiomyopathy with a floppy ventricle","Mitral valve prolapse"],"S4 is the sound of atrial contraction filling a stiff, noncompliant ventricle and is absent in atrial fibrillation."],["q-fcps-phy4-09","Which is the main determinant of the PR interval?",["Conduction delay in the AV node","Atrial repolarisation","Ventricular depolarisation","His-Purkinje conduction only"],"The PR interval is mostly the physiological delay in the AV node, which allows ventricular filling before systole."],["q-fcps-phy4-10","The delay in impulse conduction through the AV node is mainly due to:",["Slow conduction through small-diameter fibres with few gap junctions","A large number of fast sodium channels","The insulating effect of the fibrous skeleton","High intracellular calcium"],"AV nodal cells are small, have few gap junctions and depend on calcium-channel depolarisation, which slows conduction."],["q-fcps-phy4-11","A patient has a regular rhythm with the P waves unrelated to the QRS complexes and a ventricular rate of 35 beats per minute. This is:",["Third-degree heart block","Mobitz type I block","First-degree block","Sinus bradycardia"],"In complete heart block the atria and ventricles beat independently and the ventricles are driven by a slow escape rhythm."],["q-fcps-phy4-12","Which reflex increases heart rate in response to a rise in right atrial pressure?",["Bainbridge reflex","Baroreceptor reflex","Bezold-Jarisch reflex","Cushing reflex"],"Stretch of right atrial receptors increases heart rate through vagal afferents and sympathetic efferents."],["q-fcps-phy4-13","During phase II of the Valsalva manoeuvre, which change occurs?",["Venous return falls and heart rate rises","Blood pressure rises sharply","Heart rate falls","Stroke volume rises"],"Raised intrathoracic pressure reduces venous return and stroke volume, so blood pressure falls and heart rate rises reflexly."],["q-fcps-phy4-14","During dynamic exercise the greatest increase in blood flow occurs in:",["Skeletal muscle","The kidney","The gut","The skin"],"Local metabolites cause marked vasodilatation in working muscle, and flow can rise more than twentyfold."],["q-fcps-phy4-15","In a normal person standing upright, venous pooling in the legs leads to a compensatory:",["Increase in heart rate and peripheral resistance via the baroreflex","Fall in heart rate","Fall in systemic vascular resistance","Increase in cardiac preload"],"Reduced venous return lowers arterial pressure, which unloads baroreceptors and raises sympathetic outflow."],["q-fcps-phy4-16","The anatomical dead space of an adult at rest is about:",["150 mL","500 mL","50 mL","1000 mL"],"The conducting airways hold approximately 150 mL, or about 2 mL per kg of body weight."],["q-fcps-phy4-17","Minute ventilation is 6 L per minute with a tidal volume of 500 mL and a dead space of 150 mL. What is the alveolar ventilation?",["4.2 L per minute","6 L per minute","3.5 L per minute","5.5 L per minute"],"The respiratory rate is 12, and alveolar ventilation is (500 minus 150) times 12, which is 4.2 L per minute."],["q-fcps-phy4-18","Which ventilation-perfusion ratio is found at the apex of the lung in the upright position?",["High (about 3)","Low (about 0.6)","Exactly 1","Zero"],"The apex has lower perfusion than ventilation, so the ratio is high, while at the base the ratio is lower."],["q-fcps-phy4-19","In a shunt, the arterial PO2 fails to rise with 100 percent oxygen because:",["Shunted blood never contacts ventilated alveoli","Diffusion across the membrane is slow","Haemoglobin affinity is increased","Dead space is increased"],"Venous blood bypassing ventilated alveoli mixes with oxygenated blood, and extra inspired oxygen cannot correct this."],["q-fcps-phy4-20","Which type of hypoxia is present in cyanide poisoning?",["Histotoxic hypoxia","Hypoxic hypoxia","Anaemic hypoxia","Stagnant hypoxia"],"Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase, so tissues cannot use oxygen even though PaO2 and oxygen content are normal."],["q-fcps-phy4-21","Which finding is expected in anaemia with otherwise normal lungs?",["Normal PaO2 with reduced oxygen content","Low PaO2","Reduced oxygen saturation of haemoglobin","Raised alveolar-arterial gradient"],"PaO2 and saturation depend on dissolved oxygen and the lungs, but oxygen content depends on haemoglobin concentration."],["q-fcps-phy4-22","In a patient with chronic CO2 retention from COPD, giving high-flow oxygen may cause hypoventilation because:",["The hypoxic drive is removed","Central chemoreceptors become more sensitive","Oxygen directly depresses the medulla","The Hering-Breuer reflex is activated"],"Chronic hypercapnia blunts central chemoreceptor responsiveness, so peripheral chemoreceptor drive from hypoxia becomes important."],["q-fcps-phy4-23","Which change occurs on acclimatisation to high altitude?",["Increased 2,3-BPG and polycythaemia","Decreased ventilation","Metabolic acidosis from the kidney","Pulmonary vasodilatation"],"Chronic hypoxia increases erythropoietin, red cell mass and 2,3-BPG. Ventilation rises and the kidney excretes bicarbonate."],["q-fcps-phy4-24","Which effect does hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction have at high altitude?",["Pulmonary hypertension","Pulmonary vasodilatation","Reduced right ventricular afterload","Increased perfusion of poorly ventilated alveoli"],"Generalised alveolar hypoxia constricts pulmonary arterioles and raises pulmonary artery pressure, which can cause right heart strain."],["q-fcps-phy4-25","Diffusion of a gas across the alveolar membrane is proportional to all EXCEPT:",["Membrane thickness","Surface area","Partial pressure difference","Solubility of the gas"],"Fick's law makes diffusion inversely related to thickness. Area, the pressure gradient and solubility increase diffusion."],["q-fcps-phy4-26","Which gas has a diffusion-limited uptake in a healthy lung at rest?",["Carbon monoxide","Oxygen","Nitrous oxide","Carbon dioxide"],"Carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin so avidly that its partial pressure in blood stays low, so uptake depends on diffusion and is used to measure DLCO."],["q-fcps-phy4-27","In the countercurrent multiplier of the kidney, the main solute pumped out of the thick ascending limb is:",["Sodium chloride","Urea","Water","Glucose"],"The Na-K-2Cl cotransporter reabsorbs salt without water, creating the medullary osmotic gradient."],["q-fcps-phy4-28","Which segment of the nephron is permeable to urea and contributes to the medullary gradient under ADH?",["Inner medullary collecting duct","Thick ascending limb","Proximal convoluted tubule","Distal convoluted tubule"],"ADH increases urea permeability of the inner medullary collecting duct, which adds to medullary hypertonicity."],["q-fcps-phy4-29","A patient has polyuria. Urine osmolality remains low after water deprivation and rises after desmopressin. The diagnosis is:",["Central diabetes insipidus","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Primary polydipsia","SIADH"],"Rise in urine osmolality with desmopressin means the kidney responds to ADH, so the defect is lack of ADH secretion."],["q-fcps-phy4-30","SIADH is characterised by:",["Hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine","Hypernatraemia with dilute urine","Hyponatraemia with dilute urine","Hypernatraemia with hypovolaemia"],"Excess ADH causes water retention, dilutional hyponatraemia, low plasma osmolality and urine that is not maximally dilute."],["q-fcps-phy4-31","Which segment of the nephron secretes potassium under the influence of aldosterone?",["Principal cells of the collecting duct","Proximal convoluted tubule","Thin descending limb","Macula densa"],"Aldosterone increases ENaC and the Na-K-ATPase and raises potassium secretion in principal cells."],["q-fcps-phy4-32","Which acid-base disturbance results from persistent vomiting of gastric contents?",["Hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis"],"Loss of hydrochloric acid leaves bicarbonate excess, and volume depletion and hypokalaemia maintain the alkalosis."],["q-fcps-phy4-33","Which acid-base disorder is expected after a prolonged panic attack with hyperventilation?",["Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"Excess CO2 loss lowers PaCO2 and raises pH, and it can cause paraesthesia and carpopedal spasm through reduced ionised calcium."],["q-fcps-phy4-34","The anion gap is calculated as:",["Sodium minus (chloride plus bicarbonate)","Sodium plus chloride minus bicarbonate","Potassium minus chloride","Chloride minus bicarbonate"],"Normal values are about 8 to 12 mmol per litre, and the gap rises when unmeasured anions such as lactate or ketones accumulate."],["q-fcps-phy4-35","Diarrhoea causes which acid-base disturbance?",["Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis"],"Loss of bicarbonate-rich stool causes hyperchloraemic acidosis with a normal anion gap."],["q-fcps-phy4-36","In the proximal tubule, the majority of filtered bicarbonate is reabsorbed by:",["Conversion to CO2 by luminal carbonic anhydrase","Direct bicarbonate transporters on the apical membrane","Secretion of ammonium","Intercalated cells"],"Secreted H+ combines with filtered bicarbonate to form carbonic acid, which carbonic anhydrase converts to CO2 and water that diffuse into the cell."],["q-fcps-phy4-37","Which hormone is secreted by the posterior pituitary?",["Oxytocin","Prolactin","Growth hormone","ACTH"],"The posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and ADH, which are made in the hypothalamic supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei."],["q-fcps-phy4-38","Growth hormone secretion is stimulated by:",["Hypoglycaemia","Hyperglycaemia","Somatostatin","Obesity"],"Sleep, exercise, hypoglycaemia, fasting and amino acids stimulate GH, while glucose, somatostatin and IGF-1 inhibit it."],["q-fcps-phy4-39","Which test is used to confirm growth hormone excess in acromegaly?",["Failure of GH suppression after an oral glucose load","Insulin tolerance test","Short synacthen test","Water deprivation test"],"Normally glucose suppresses GH. In acromegaly it fails to suppress, and IGF-1 is raised."],["q-fcps-phy4-40","The effect of thyroid hormone on the heart is to:",["Increase heart rate and contractility","Decrease beta-receptor number","Prolong PR interval","Decrease cardiac output"],"Thyroid hormone increases beta-adrenergic receptor expression and cardiac output, so hyperthyroidism causes tachycardia and atrial fibrillation."],["q-fcps-phy4-41","Which iodine-trapping step is the first in thyroid hormone synthesis?",["Sodium-iodide symporter uptake on the basolateral membrane","Coupling of MIT and DIT","Proteolysis of thyroglobulin","Deiodination of T4"],"Iodide is actively concentrated in follicular cells by the sodium-iodide symporter, which is the basis of radioiodine therapy."],["q-fcps-phy4-42","Which is the most potent natural form of vitamin D?",["1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","25-hydroxycholecalciferol","Cholecalciferol","24,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol"],"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol) is made in the proximal tubule by 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which is stimulated by PTH."],["q-fcps-phy4-43","A rise in plasma phosphate causes which change in calcium regulation?",["Reduced ionised calcium and increased PTH secretion","Increased calcitriol","Reduced PTH","Increased calcitonin only"],"High phosphate binds calcium and inhibits 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which contributes to secondary hyperparathyroidism in renal failure."],["q-fcps-phy4-44","Which of these is a metabolic effect of insulin?",["Stimulates glycogen synthesis and lipogenesis","Stimulates gluconeogenesis","Stimulates lipolysis","Stimulates ketogenesis"],"Insulin promotes storage of glucose and fat and inhibits hepatic glucose output, lipolysis and ketone formation."],["q-fcps-phy4-45","Glucagon increases blood glucose mainly by:",["Stimulating hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis","Increasing peripheral glucose uptake","Inhibiting hepatic gluconeogenesis","Stimulating insulin release"],"Glucagon acts through cAMP in hepatocytes and has little effect on skeletal muscle glycogen."],["q-fcps-phy4-46","In the follicular phase of the menstrual cycle, rising oestrogen causes:",["Negative feedback on FSH until the mid-cycle positive feedback on LH","Suppression of LH throughout","Secretion of progesterone by granulosa cells","Ovulation by direct action on the follicle"],"Moderate oestrogen inhibits FSH and LH, but sustained high oestrogen for about 48 hours switches to positive feedback and triggers the LH surge."],["q-fcps-phy4-47","Progesterone causes a rise in basal body temperature by acting on the:",["Hypothalamus","Ovary","Pituitary","Uterus"],"Progesterone resets the hypothalamic thermoregulatory set point upwards, producing a biphasic temperature chart."],["q-fcps-phy4-48","Which hormone is responsible for milk ejection?",["Oxytocin","Prolactin","Oestrogen","Human placental lactogen"],"Suckling stimulates oxytocin release, which contracts myoepithelial cells. Prolactin stimulates milk synthesis."],["q-fcps-phy4-49","A healthy 9-month-old infant still shows a brisk Moro reflex. This suggests:",["Failure of cortical inhibition of a primitive brainstem reflex","Normal development","Spinal muscular atrophy","Cerebellar disease"],"Primitive reflexes such as the Moro reflex are brainstem-mediated and normally disappear by about 4 to 6 months as the cortex matures."],["q-fcps-phy4-50","An inhibitory postsynaptic potential is produced by opening of which channels?",["Chloride channels","Sodium channels","Calcium channels","Non-selective cation channels"],"Chloride influx or potassium efflux hyperpolarises the postsynaptic membrane and moves it away from threshold."],["q-fcps-phy4-51","Which neurotransmitter is the main inhibitory transmitter in the brain?",["GABA","Glutamate","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"],"GABA acts on GABA-A receptors (chloride channels), which are enhanced by benzodiazepines."],["q-fcps-phy4-52","Temporal summation at a synapse is the result of:",["Rapidly repeated stimulation from a single presynaptic neuron","Simultaneous stimulation from several neurons","Presynaptic inhibition","Long-term potentiation"],"When potentials arrive before the previous one decays, they add up to reach threshold."],["q-fcps-phy4-53","The muscle spindle detects:",["Muscle length and the rate of change in length","Muscle tension","Joint pain","Temperature"],"Spindles lie in parallel with extrafusal fibres and drive the stretch reflex. Golgi tendon organs sense tension."],["q-fcps-phy4-54","Which structure is the integrating centre for the stretch reflex?",["Spinal cord","Cerebellum","Thalamus","Basal ganglia"],"The monosynaptic stretch reflex is processed in the spinal cord, with gamma motor neurons modulating spindle sensitivity."],["q-fcps-phy4-55","A patient has an intention tremor, dysmetria and past-pointing on the right. The lesion is in the:",["Right cerebellar hemisphere","Left cerebellar hemisphere","Right basal ganglia","Left red nucleus only"],"Cerebellar hemispheric lesions cause ipsilateral limb ataxia because the pathways cross twice."],["q-fcps-phy4-56","Parkinson disease is characterised by loss of dopaminergic neurons in the:",["Substantia nigra pars compacta","Caudate nucleus","Subthalamic nucleus","Globus pallidus externa"],"Loss of nigrostriatal dopamine reduces direct-pathway activity, causing bradykinesia, rigidity and resting tremor."],["q-fcps-phy4-57","Which stage of sleep has the highest proportion of slow, high-amplitude delta waves?",["Stage N3 (slow-wave sleep)","REM sleep","Stage N1","Stage N2"],"Slow-wave sleep is deepest and is when growth hormone secretion peaks and sleepwalking and night terrors occur."],["q-fcps-phy4-58","During REM sleep, which finding is characteristic?",["Muscle atonia with a desynchronised EEG","High muscle tone","Delta waves","Reduced brain oxygen consumption"],"REM sleep shows low-voltage fast EEG activity, rapid eye movements and loss of postural muscle tone."],["q-fcps-phy4-59","The thermoregulatory centre that responds to a rise in core temperature is in the:",["Anterior hypothalamus","Posterior hypothalamus","Medulla","Thalamus"],"The preoptic anterior hypothalamus activates heat-loss mechanisms. The posterior hypothalamus drives heat conservation and production."],["q-fcps-phy4-60","Pyrogens such as IL-1 cause fever by:",["Raising the hypothalamic set point through prostaglandin E2","Damaging the hypothalamus","Directly increasing muscle heat production","Blocking sweating"],"Cytokines induce PGE2 in the preoptic area, which resets the set point. Antipyretics such as paracetamol and aspirin inhibit cyclooxygenase."],["q-fcps-phy4-61","Fast, well-localised sharp pain is carried by which fibres?",["A-delta fibres","C fibres","A-alpha fibres","B fibres"],"A-delta fibres are thin and myelinated and carry sharp pain. C fibres carry slow, dull, burning pain."],["q-fcps-phy4-62","The gate control theory of pain proposes that pain transmission in the dorsal horn can be reduced by activity in:",["Large A-beta touch fibres","C fibres","A-delta fibres only","Sympathetic fibres"],"Large-fibre touch input activates inhibitory interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa, which is the basis of rubbing a painful area and of TENS."],["q-fcps-phy4-63","In the length-tension relationship of skeletal muscle, maximal active tension is developed at:",["The optimal sarcomere length of about 2.2 micrometres","Fully stretched sarcomeres","Fully shortened sarcomeres","Any length equally"],"At optimal length there is maximal overlap of actin and myosin cross-bridges."],["q-fcps-phy4-64","Tetanus in skeletal muscle (fused contraction) occurs when:",["Stimuli arrive faster than the muscle can relax between twitches","Stimuli are weak","Calcium is depleted","ATP is abundant"],"At high stimulation frequencies calcium remains elevated and twitches summate into a smooth sustained contraction."],["q-fcps-phy4-65","Rigor mortis occurs because:",["ATP is depleted so myosin heads cannot detach from actin","Calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Troponin is permanently inactive","Myosin ATPase is overactive"],"ATP is needed for detachment of cross-bridges, and after death its absence locks the muscle."],["q-fcps-phy4-66","Which fibre type is fatigue-resistant and rich in mitochondria and myoglobin?",["Type I (slow oxidative)","Type IIb (fast glycolytic)","Type IIx","Cardiac fibres only"],"Type I fibres are red, aerobic and used for postural muscles and endurance."],["q-fcps-phy4-67","Which blood group is the universal plasma donor?",["AB","O","A","B"],"AB plasma contains no anti-A or anti-B antibodies, so it is compatible with all recipients. Group O red cells are the universal red cell donor."],["q-fcps-phy4-68","Haemolytic disease of the newborn due to Rh incompatibility typically affects:",["The second Rh-positive child of an Rh-negative mother","The first child of an Rh-negative mother","An Rh-positive mother's child","Any child of an O-group mother"],"Sensitisation occurs at the first delivery, and IgG anti-D then crosses the placenta in later pregnancies. Anti-D immunoglobulin prevents it."],["q-fcps-phy4-69","Which clotting factor is deficient in classic haemophilia A?",["Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor XI","Factor VII"],"Haemophilia A is X-linked factor VIII deficiency with a prolonged aPTT and a normal PT."],["q-fcps-phy4-70","A prolonged prothrombin time with a normal aPTT suggests a defect in the:",["Extrinsic pathway (factor VII)","Intrinsic pathway","Common pathway only","Platelet function"],"PT tests the extrinsic and common pathways, and it is the first to prolong in early warfarin therapy because factor VII has the shortest half-life."],["q-fcps-phy4-71","The main anticoagulant action of antithrombin is to inhibit:",["Thrombin and factor Xa","Factor VIII","Fibrinogen","von Willebrand factor"],"Antithrombin is activated by heparin and inactivates thrombin, factor Xa and other serine proteases."],["q-fcps-phy4-72","Which cell secretes the hormone erythropoietin in response to hypoxia?",["Peritubular interstitial fibroblasts of the renal cortex","Juxtaglomerular cells","Podocytes","Macula densa cells"],"Peritubular cells sense hypoxia through HIF and release EPO. Chronic kidney disease therefore causes anaemia."],["q-fcps-phy5-01","Which ion current is responsible for the spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation of sinoatrial nodal cells?",["The funny current (If), a mixed Na+/K+ inward current","Fast sodium current","Delayed rectifier potassium current","Chloride current"],"Hyperpolarisation-activated channels (HCN) open at the end of repolarisation and slowly depolarise the cell, and ivabradine blocks them."],["q-fcps-phy5-02","Parasympathetic stimulation of the SA node slows the heart rate by:",["Increasing K+ conductance and reducing the slope of phase 4","Increasing If","Increasing calcium influx","Shortening phase 0"],"Acetylcholine acting on M2 receptors opens K+ channels and hyperpolarises the cell, so it takes longer to reach threshold."],["q-fcps-phy5-03","According to the Starling principle, which force favours movement of fluid out of the capillary?",["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Plasma oncotic pressure","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Lymphatic drainage"],"Capillary hydrostatic pressure and interstitial oncotic pressure favour filtration, and plasma oncotic pressure opposes it."],["q-fcps-phy5-04","Which of the following is a cause of oedema through reduced plasma oncotic pressure?",["Cirrhosis with hypoalbuminaemia","Right heart failure","Venous obstruction","Lymphatic obstruction"],"Low albumin from impaired synthesis, and loss in nephrotic syndrome, lowers the opposing force and allows fluid to leave the capillaries."],["q-fcps-phy5-05","Lymphoedema is characterised by oedema rich in:",["Protein","Red cells","Glucose only","Water with no protein"],"Blocked lymphatics prevent return of the filtered protein, so the interstitial oncotic pressure rises and perpetuates oedema."],["q-fcps-phy5-06","Cerebral blood flow is most sensitive to changes in:",["Arterial PCO2","Arterial PO2 over the normal range","Blood glucose","Plasma sodium"],"Hypercapnia causes cerebral vasodilatation and hypocapnia from hyperventilation causes vasoconstriction and light-headedness."],["q-fcps-phy5-07","Autoregulation of renal blood flow keeps flow constant over a mean arterial pressure range of about:",["80 to 180 mmHg","40 to 80 mmHg","180 to 260 mmHg","20 to 60 mmHg"],"The myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback maintain renal blood flow and GFR."],["q-fcps-phy5-08","In the early stage of haemorrhagic shock, which compensatory response occurs?",["Increased sympathetic activity with vasoconstriction and tachycardia","Decreased ADH secretion","Decreased renin release","Vasodilatation of the splanchnic bed"],"Baroreceptor unloading increases sympathetic outflow, and renin, ADH and angiotensin II help restore volume and pressure."],["q-fcps-phy5-09","Total body water in an adult male is about what percentage of body weight?",["60%","40%","20%","75%"],"About two-thirds is intracellular (40 percent of weight) and one-third is extracellular (20 percent), of which plasma forms 5 percent."],["q-fcps-phy5-10","Which substance can be used to measure the extracellular fluid volume?",["Inulin","Deuterium oxide","Evans blue","Radioiodinated albumin"],"Inulin and mannitol distribute through extracellular fluid, and Evans blue and labelled albumin measure plasma volume."],["q-fcps-phy5-11","Plasma osmolality is approximately:",["2 x [Na+] + glucose/18 + urea/2.8 (mmol/L, with glucose and urea in mg/dL)","[Na+] only","[Na+] + [K+] only","Twice the potassium"],"Sodium and its anions account for most of the osmolality of 285 to 295 mOsm/kg, and glucose and urea contribute the rest."],["q-fcps-phy5-12","A patient with severe hyperglycaemia has a measured serum sodium of 128 mmol/L. The low sodium is because of:",["Water shifting out of cells into the extracellular fluid","Sodium loss in urine","SIADH","Hypoaldosteronism"],"Glucose is an osmotically active solute, so water leaves the cells and dilutes the plasma sodium. Sodium falls about 1.6 mmol/L per 100 mg/dL rise in glucose."],["q-fcps-phy5-13","Following infusion of isotonic saline, which compartment expands?",["Extracellular fluid only","Intracellular fluid only","Both compartments equally","Plasma only"],"There is no osmotic gradient, so the fluid distributes through the extracellular space, and about 25 percent stays in the vessels."],["q-fcps-phy5-14","Which segment of the nephron reabsorbs the greatest proportion of filtered water?",["Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct"],"About 65 percent of the filtrate is reabsorbed isosmotically in the proximal tubule."],["q-fcps-phy5-15","The filtration fraction is calculated as:",["GFR divided by renal plasma flow","Renal blood flow divided by cardiac output","GFR divided by renal blood flow","Urine flow divided by GFR"],"The normal value is about 20 percent, and efferent constriction by angiotensin II raises it."],["q-fcps-phy5-16","Constriction of the efferent arteriole by angiotensin II causes:",["Increased GFR with decreased renal blood flow","Decreased GFR","Increased renal blood flow","No change in GFR"],"It raises glomerular capillary pressure, but it also lowers renal plasma flow. ACE inhibitors dilate the efferent arteriole."],["q-fcps-phy5-17","Which of these will reduce the glomerular filtration rate?",["Obstruction of the ureter","Afferent arteriole dilatation","Reduced plasma oncotic pressure","Efferent arteriole constriction"],"Ureteric obstruction increases Bowman's capsule hydrostatic pressure, which opposes filtration."],["q-fcps-phy5-18","The renal threshold for glucose is about:",["180 mg/dL","100 mg/dL","300 mg/dL","60 mg/dL"],"Above this plasma level the transport maximum is exceeded and glucose appears in urine, at about 10 mmol/L."],["q-fcps-phy5-19","A patient has normal blood glucose but glucosuria. The most likely explanation is:",["Defective SGLT2 (renal glycosuria)","Insulin deficiency","Deficient GLUT2 in beta cells","Raised GFR"],"Familial renal glycosuria is a benign defect of proximal tubular glucose reabsorption."],["q-fcps-phy5-20","Which diuretic acts on the early distal convoluted tubule to inhibit the Na-Cl cotransporter?",["Hydrochlorothiazide","Furosemide","Spironolactone","Acetazolamide"],"Thiazides inhibit NCC and increase calcium reabsorption."],["q-fcps-phy5-21","Which of these will occur in the compensation for chronic respiratory acidosis?",["Increased renal bicarbonate reabsorption","Decreased PaCO2","Increased renal bicarbonate excretion","Hyperventilation"],"The kidney retains HCO3-, raising it by about 3.5 to 4 mmol/L for each 10 mmHg rise in PaCO2 over days."],["q-fcps-phy5-22","The expected PaCO2 in metabolic acidosis can be estimated by Winter's formula as:",["1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8","[HCO3-] + 15","0.7 x [HCO3-]","40 minus [HCO3-]"],"If the measured PaCO2 is higher than expected there is a coexisting respiratory acidosis."],["q-fcps-phy5-23","In metabolic acidosis, the respiratory response is:",["Kussmaul breathing with increased ventilation","Hypoventilation","Cheyne-Stokes breathing","No change"],"H+ stimulates peripheral and central chemoreceptors and increases the depth of respiration."],["q-fcps-phy5-24","Which hormone increases the number of aquaporin-2 channels through V2 receptors and cAMP?",["Antidiuretic hormone","Aldosterone","Atrial natriuretic peptide","Angiotensin II"],"ADH raises permeability of the collecting duct to water, so water is reabsorbed down the medullary gradient."],["q-fcps-phy5-25","Which is a major stimulus for ADH secretion?",["Rise in plasma osmolality of 1 to 2 percent","Fall in plasma osmolality","Alcohol","Atrial stretch"],"Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus are very sensitive, and large falls in blood volume also stimulate ADH."],["q-fcps-phy5-26","Lung compliance is greatest at:",["Mid-lung volumes (the steep part of the pressure-volume curve)","Residual volume","Total lung capacity","Equally at all volumes"],"At low volumes and near total lung capacity the curve is flat, so compliance is lower."],["q-fcps-phy5-27","The hysteresis in the lung's pressure-volume curve is due mainly to:",["Surface tension and the opening of collapsed alveoli","Muscle fatigue","Changes in airway resistance","Blood flow"],"A higher pressure is needed to inflate the lung from collapse than is released on deflation."],["q-fcps-phy5-28","Which factor is the major site of resistance in the airways in health?",["Medium-sized bronchi","Small bronchioles below 2 mm","Trachea","Alveolar ducts"],"The parallel arrangement of numerous small airways gives them a low total resistance."],["q-fcps-phy5-29","The functional residual capacity is the volume of air in the lungs at:",["The end of a normal expiration","The end of a maximal inspiration","The end of a maximal expiration","The end of a normal inspiration"],"FRC is the balance point between the inward recoil of the lung and the outward recoil of the chest wall."],["q-fcps-phy5-30","In emphysema, the functional residual capacity is:",["Increased because of loss of elastic recoil","Decreased","Unchanged","Normal with a raised FEV1"],"Loss of elastic tissue allows the chest wall to expand and the lungs hyperinflate, and the lung compliance rises."],["q-fcps-phy5-31","A flow-volume loop with a flattened plateau on both inspiration and expiration suggests:",["Fixed upper airway obstruction","Asthma","Emphysema","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"A fixed obstruction, such as tracheal stenosis, limits flow in both phases."],["q-fcps-phy5-32","The carotid body glomus cells respond to a:",["Fall in PaO2, rise in PaCO2 and fall in pH","Rise in PaO2","Fall in PaCO2","Rise in pH"],"They are the main peripheral chemoreceptors for hypoxia, and they respond to the PaO2 rather than oxygen content."],["q-fcps-phy5-33","The P50 of adult haemoglobin is approximately:",["26 mmHg","40 mmHg","10 mmHg","60 mmHg"],"P50 is the PO2 at which haemoglobin is 50 percent saturated, and a higher P50 means lower affinity."],["q-fcps-phy5-34","Fetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult haemoglobin because it:",["Binds 2,3-BPG poorly","Has more haem groups","Has a higher iron content","Is a monomer"],"HbF (alpha2 gamma2) binds 2,3-BPG less avidly, so its curve is shifted to the left, which favours oxygen transfer across the placenta."],["q-fcps-phy5-35","A patient has oxygen saturation of 98 percent but a haemoglobin of 6 g/dL. The oxygen content is:",["Markedly reduced","Normal","Slightly increased","Dependent only on PaO2"],"Content is mainly Hb x 1.34 x saturation, so severe anaemia gives low content despite a normal PaO2 and saturation."],["q-fcps-phy5-36","Which gastrointestinal hormone is the most potent stimulant of pancreatic enzyme secretion?",["Cholecystokinin","Secretin","Gastrin","Somatostatin"],"CCK from I cells responds to fat and amino acids, and it contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi."],["q-fcps-phy5-37","Secretin stimulates the pancreas to secrete:",["A bicarbonate-rich fluid","Enzyme-rich fluid","Insulin","Mucus"],"Acid in the duodenum releases secretin, which neutralises acid and provides the optimum pH for pancreatic enzymes."],["q-fcps-phy5-38","Which phase of gastric secretion accounts for the largest proportion of acid output after a meal?",["Gastric phase","Cephalic phase","Intestinal phase","Interdigestive phase"],"Distension and peptides stimulate gastrin and vagal reflexes, producing about 60 percent of the acid."],["q-fcps-phy5-39","Which cells of the gastric mucosa secrete pepsinogen?",["Chief cells","Parietal cells","G cells","Mucous neck cells"],"Pepsinogen is activated to pepsin by acid and by pepsin itself."],["q-fcps-phy5-40","The proton pump of parietal cells is the:",["H+/K+ ATPase","Na+/K+ ATPase","Ca2+ ATPase","Na+/H+ exchanger"],"Omeprazole irreversibly inhibits this pump, and the arrival of cell stimulation by histamine, gastrin and acetylcholine converge on it."],["q-fcps-phy5-41","Intrinsic factor is secreted by:",["Parietal cells","Chief cells","G cells","D cells"],"It is needed for absorption of vitamin B12 in the terminal ileum."],["q-fcps-phy5-42","The slow waves of the gastrointestinal tract are generated by the:",["Interstitial cells of Cajal","Enteric neurons","Smooth muscle cells themselves","Mast cells"],"These pacemaker cells set the basic electrical rhythm, which is about 3 per minute in the stomach and 12 per minute in the duodenum."],["q-fcps-phy5-43","The migrating motor complex:",["Sweeps undigested material from the stomach and small bowel in the fasting state","Is stimulated by a meal","Is triggered by gastrin","Produces mass movements in the colon"],"It recurs every 90 minutes during fasting and is stimulated by motilin."],["q-fcps-phy5-44","Which reflex stimulates colonic mass movement after eating?",["Gastrocolic reflex","Enterogastric reflex","Rectosphincteric reflex","Peristaltic reflex"],"Gastric distension triggers contractions in the colon, which are mediated by gastrin and the autonomic nerves."],["q-fcps-phy5-45","Absence of the ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus in the rectum causes:",["Failure of relaxation of the internal sphincter and distal obstruction","Diarrhoea","Increased peristalsis","Faecal incontinence"],"This is Hirschsprung disease, and the rectoanal inhibitory reflex is absent."],["q-fcps-phy5-46","Oral rehydration solution works because:",["Glucose-coupled sodium absorption occurs through SGLT1 in the intestine even in secretory diarrhoea","It inhibits chloride secretion directly","It increases gut motility","It binds toxins"],"SGLT1 cotransports sodium and glucose, and water follows osmotically."],["q-fcps-phy5-47","The majority of the bile acids in the enterohepatic circulation are reabsorbed by:",["Active transport in the terminal ileum","Passive diffusion in the colon","Active transport in the jejunum","Passive diffusion in the stomach"],"About 95 percent are recycled, and terminal ileal resection reduces the bile salt pool and causes steatorrhoea."],["q-fcps-phy5-48","Which of the following is absorbed by the sodium-dependent glucose transporter SGLT1 in the small intestine?",["Glucose and galactose","Fructose","Lactose","Sucrose"],"Fructose is absorbed by GLUT5 through facilitated diffusion."],["q-fcps-phy5-49","Which enzyme converts trypsinogen to trypsin in the duodenum?",["Enterokinase (enteropeptidase)","Pepsin","Chymotrypsin","Carboxypeptidase"],"Brush border enterokinase activates trypsinogen, and trypsin then activates the other pancreatic proenzymes."],["q-fcps-phy5-50","The cephalic phase of salivary secretion is mediated by the:",["Parasympathetic nervous system","Sympathetic nervous system","Gastrin","Secretin"],"Salivary secretion is controlled almost entirely by the autonomic nerves, and parasympathetic stimulation gives a profuse, watery saliva."],["q-fcps-phy5-51","Which hormone is mainly responsible for the stimulation of gluconeogenesis during prolonged fasting?",["Cortisol and glucagon","Insulin","Growth hormone only","Oxytocin"],"Cortisol permits and enhances the actions of glucagon, and it raises substrate by protein breakdown."],["q-fcps-phy5-52","Cortisol has which of the following actions?",["Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects","Hypoglycaemic effect","Increased protein synthesis in muscle","Decreased gluconeogenesis"],"It stabilises lysosomes, reduces cytokine production, and causes lymphopenia and eosinopenia."],["q-fcps-phy5-53","Aldosterone secretion is stimulated chiefly by:",["Angiotensin II and a rise in plasma potassium","ACTH only","A rise in plasma sodium","ANP"],"Hyperkalaemia directly stimulates the zona glomerulosa."],["q-fcps-phy5-54","Which test is used to distinguish pituitary from ectopic ACTH in Cushing syndrome?",["High-dose dexamethasone suppression test","Low-dose dexamethasone test","Short synacthen test","Insulin tolerance test"],"Pituitary adenomas retain partial feedback and suppress with high-dose dexamethasone, while ectopic sources usually do not."],["q-fcps-phy5-55","Testosterone in the male is secreted by:",["Leydig cells under LH","Sertoli cells under FSH","Seminiferous tubules","Epididymis"],"Sertoli cells support spermatogenesis, and they secrete inhibin and androgen-binding protein."],["q-fcps-phy5-56","Inhibin acts to:",["Selectively inhibit FSH secretion","Inhibit LH","Stimulate testosterone","Stimulate spermatogenesis"],"Inhibin B from Sertoli cells and granulosa cells provides negative feedback on FSH."],["q-fcps-phy5-57","Human chorionic gonadotropin in early pregnancy resembles which pituitary hormone in action?",["LH","FSH","Prolactin","GH"],"hCG maintains the corpus luteum, and it is detected in urine pregnancy tests."],["q-fcps-phy5-58","During the nerve action potential, repolarisation is due to:",["Opening of voltage-gated K+ channels and inactivation of Na+ channels","Closure of potassium channels","Opening of chloride channels","Na+/K+ ATPase alone"],"Delayed potassium efflux and sodium channel inactivation return the membrane towards the resting potential."],["q-fcps-phy5-59","Which factor increases the conduction velocity of a nerve fibre?",["Myelination and increased fibre diameter","Cooling","Local anaesthetic","Decreased diameter"],"Saltatory conduction at the nodes of Ranvier speeds conduction, and demyelination slows it."],["q-fcps-phy5-60","During the absolute refractory period of a neuron:",["No stimulus, however strong, can produce another action potential","A strong stimulus can produce a response","The membrane is hyperpolarised","Potassium channels are closed"],"It is due to inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels, and it ensures one-way propagation."],["q-fcps-phy5-61","Hyperkalaemia causes muscle weakness because:",["Depolarisation inactivates sodium channels","It hyperpolarises the membrane","It blocks acetylcholine release","It blocks calcium channels directly"],"A persistently raised resting potential inactivates the voltage-gated Na+ channels, and this reduces excitability."],["q-fcps-phy5-62","Which receptor type adapts rapidly?",["Pacinian corpuscle","Merkel disc","Ruffini ending","Muscle spindle (static)"],"Rapidly adapting receptors detect vibration and changes, while slowly adapting receptors signal sustained pressure."],["q-fcps-phy5-63","In phototransduction, light causes:",["Hyperpolarisation of the photoreceptor by closure of cGMP-gated channels","Depolarisation of rods","Opening of sodium channels","Increase in glutamate release"],"Photoactivation of rhodopsin activates transducin and phosphodiesterase, which lowers cGMP."],["q-fcps-phy5-64","Dark adaptation is delayed in:",["Vitamin A deficiency","Vitamin C deficiency","Vitamin K deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"Rhodopsin regeneration needs vitamin A, and its deficiency produces night blindness."],["q-fcps-phy5-65","Cones are responsible for:",["Colour vision and high acuity in bright light","Night vision","Peripheral vision","Light adaptation only"],"The fovea contains only cones, and the rods are most dense in the periphery."],["q-fcps-phy5-66","The sensation of high-pitched sounds is detected at which part of the basilar membrane?",["Base of the cochlea","Apex of the cochlea","Helicotrema","Middle of the cochlea"],"The base is narrow and stiff, so it vibrates at high frequencies, and the apex is wide and floppy, so it responds to low frequencies."],["q-fcps-phy5-67","Conductive hearing loss is suggested when the Rinne test shows:",["Bone conduction louder than air conduction","Air conduction louder than bone conduction","Both heard equally with no difference","Sound heard in the better ear in the Weber test only"],"In sensorineural loss air conduction remains better than bone conduction, and the Weber test lateralises to the better ear."],["q-fcps-phy5-68","Hair cells of the cochlea are depolarised by:",["K+ influx from the endolymph when the stereocilia bend","Na+ influx from the perilymph","Cl- efflux","Ca2+ efflux"],"Endolymph is rich in potassium, and bending opens mechanically gated channels."],["q-fcps-phy5-69","An upper motor neuron lesion typically produces:",["Spasticity, hyperreflexia and an extensor plantar response","Flaccid paralysis with fasciculations","Muscle wasting","Hyporeflexia"],"A lower motor neuron lesion produces weakness with wasting, fasciculations, hypotonia and reduced reflexes."],["q-fcps-phy5-70","The crossed extensor reflex helps to:",["Maintain balance when one limb is withdrawn from a painful stimulus","Relax both limbs","Elicit the stretch reflex","Inhibit the flexor withdrawal"],"The contralateral extensor muscles contract to support the body weight."],["q-fcps-phy5-71","Alpha waves on the EEG are typical of:",["A relaxed awake person with eyes closed","Deep sleep","Active concentration with eyes open","REM sleep"],"They are 8 to 13 Hz and are best recorded over the occipital region, while beta waves (above 13 Hz) accompany alertness."],["q-fcps-phy5-72","Which receptor type mediates the sympathetic vasoconstriction of skin arterioles?",["Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors","Beta-2 adrenergic receptors","Muscarinic receptors","Dopamine receptors"],"Skeletal muscle arterioles have beta-2 receptors, which cause vasodilatation with adrenaline."],["q-fcps-phy5-73","Parasympathetic stimulation causes:",["Contraction of the detrusor muscle and relaxation of the internal sphincter","Bronchodilatation","Tachycardia","Mydriasis"],"Acetylcholine acts on M3 receptors to empty the bladder, and sympathetic stimulation relaxes the detrusor via beta-3 receptors."],["q-fcps-phy5-74","Haemoglobin synthesis requires:",["Iron, globin and protoporphyrin","Vitamin B12 only","Copper only","Folate only"],"Haem is formed from protoporphyrin and iron, and the globin chains are synthesised on ribosomes."],["q-fcps-phy5-75","The lifespan of a red blood cell is about:",["120 days","30 days","60 days","200 days"],"Old red cells are removed by the macrophages of the spleen, and haem is converted to bilirubin."],["q-fcps-phy5-76","Which cytokine is the main stimulus for megakaryocyte differentiation and platelet production?",["Thrombopoietin","Erythropoietin","G-CSF","Interleukin-2"],"Thrombopoietin is made mainly in the liver."],["q-fcps-phy5-77","Platelet adhesion to exposed subendothelial collagen is mediated by:",["von Willebrand factor and glycoprotein Ib","Fibrinogen and GP IIb/IIIa","Thromboxane A2","ADP"],"Adhesion is followed by aggregation through GP IIb/IIIa and fibrinogen."],["q-fcps-phy5-78","Which substance is the main inhibitor of platelet aggregation released by normal endothelium?",["Prostacyclin (PGI2)","Thromboxane A2","ADP","Serotonin"],"Prostacyclin and nitric oxide inhibit platelet activation, and thromboxane A2 promotes it."],["q-fcps-phy5-79","Plasmin breaks down fibrin, producing which marker?",["D-dimer","Fibrinogen","Thrombin","Antithrombin"],"D-dimers are cross-linked fibrin degradation products and are raised in thrombosis."]];
